	\section*{The Outline}
	These notes will develop and motivate the intrinsic calculus of tensor fields over smooth manifolds alongside related concepts from differential geometry. Prerequisites include, but are not limited to set theory, linear algebra, and vector calculus. The Einstein summation convention will be utilized throughout.
	
	\section*{Topological Spaces}
	In order to establish the notion of a manifold, some groundwork needs to be established first. Let $M\neq\varnothing$ be a collection of elements. If $p\in M$, then $p$ is said to be a point in $M$. Furthermore, $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{P}(M)$ is said to be a topology of $M$ if it satisfies the following properties (axioms):
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $\varnothing,M\in\mathcal{T}$ ------------------------------------------------------------ Nothing \& Everything -----------------------------
		\item $\forall I\subseteq\mathbb{N},\,\left\{U_i\right\}_{i\in I}\subseteq\mathcal{T}\implies\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i\in\mathcal{T}$ --------------------- Closure Under Unions ------------------------------
		\item $\forall n\in\mathbb{N},\,\left\{U_i\right\}_{i=1}^n\subseteq\mathcal{T}\implies\bigcap_{i=1}^nU_i\in\mathcal{T}$ --------------------- Closure Under Finite Intersections --------------
	\end{itemize}
	\vspace{1 mm}
	The pairing $(M,\mathcal{T})$ is said to be a topological space, and any element of $\mathcal{T}$ is said to be an open subset of $M$ by definition. Now, given any $p\in M$, let $\mathcal{N}(p)=\left\{U\in\mathcal{T}\;|\;p\in U\right\}$ denote the neighborhood system of the point $p$. If $U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$, then $U$ is called a neighborhood of $p$. The following notions can now be formalized:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $p$ is an interior point of $S\subseteq M$ (i.e. $p\in \mathrm{Int}(S)$) if $\exists\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$ such that $U\subseteq S$.
		\item $p$ is an exterior point of $S\subseteq M$ (i.e. $p\in\mathrm{Ext}(S)$) if $\exists\,U\subseteq \mathcal{N}(p)$ such that $U\subseteq M\setminus S$.
		\item $p$ is a boundary point of $S\subseteq M$ (i.e. $p\in\partial S$) if $\forall\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p),\,U\,\cap\,S\neq\varnothing$ and $U\,\cap\,(M\setminus S)\neq\varnothing$.
		\item $p$ is an accumulation point of $S\subseteq M$ (i.e. $p\in S'$) if $\forall\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p),\,U\setminus\{p\}\,\cap\,S\neq\varnothing$.
	\end{itemize}
	The closure of any such $S\subseteq M$ is thus defined by $\mathrm{Cl}(S)=\mathrm{Int}(S)\,\cup\,\partial S$, and without proof, it can be shown that $\mathrm{Cl}(S)=S\,\cup\,S'$. It is said that $S$ is closed if $S=\mathrm{Cl}(S)$. It likewise follows that $S$ is open if $S=\mathrm{Int}(S)$. These definitions clearly partition all the regions of $M$ with respect to any one of its subsets.
	
