	\section*{The Hausdorff Property}
	With the established structure at hand, $(M,\mathcal{T})$ is considered Hausdorff if $\forall\,p\neq q,\,\exists\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$ and $\exists\,V\in\mathcal{N}(q)$ such that $U\cap V=\varnothing$. In other words, any two distinct points in $M$ can be located respectively in their own neighborhoods which do not overlap with each other.
	
