	Let $s:\mathbb{N}\to M$ be a sequence. By definition, $s$ converges to $p\in M$ (i.e. $s\to p$) if $\forall\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p),\,\exists\,m\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $n\geq m\implies s_n\in U$. Intuitively, no matter how small the neighborhood $U$ is around $p$, there always exists a location in the sequence $s$ beyond which the outputs of $s$ reside entirely inside $U$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Assuming $(M,\mathcal{T})$ is Hausdorff, suppose that the sequence $s$ converges to both $p,q\in M$. There necessarily exists open sets $U,V\subset M$ such that $p\in U$ and $q\in V$. It directly follows that $\exists\,m\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $n\geq m\implies s_n\in U\,\cap\, V$. Thus, by assumption, if $p\neq q$, then one can choose $U,V$ such that $U\,\cap\, V=\varnothing$, which would be a contradiction, implying $p=q$. That is to say, convergent limits of sequences are unique.
	
	\section*{Topological Basis}
	Given the topological space $(M,\mathcal{T})$, a collection of open subsets $\mathcal{B}\in\mathcal{T}$ is called a basis of the topology $\mathcal{T}$ if $\forall\,U\mathcal{\in\mathcal{T}},\,\exists\,\left\{U_i\right\}_{i\in I}\subseteq\mathcal{B}$ such that $\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i=U$, where $I\subseteq\mathbb{N}$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	The topological space $(M,\mathcal{T})$ is said to be first-countable if $\forall\,p\in M,\,\exists\,U_k\subseteq U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$, with $k\in\mathbb{N}$, such that $\mathcal{N}(p)=\left\{U_k\right\}_{k\in I}$, where $I\subseteq\mathbb{N}$. In other words, in this context, every neighborhood system can be generated by a countable set of neighborhoods. This set is referred to as a countable neighborhood basis. 
	
