	\section*{Topological Continuity}
	Suppose $(M,\mathcal{T}_M)$ and $(N,\mathcal{T}_N)$ are topological spaces. The map $F:M\to N$ is called continuous (with respect to $\mathcal{T}_M$ and $\mathcal{T}_N$) if $V\in\mathcal{T}_N\implies F^{-1}(V)\in\mathcal{T}_M$, where $F^{-1}$ is the preimage of $F$. That is to say, for any $p\in M$, fixing the neighborhood $V\in\mathcal{N}(F(p))$ guarantees the existence of another neighborhood $U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$ such that $F(U)\subseteq V$. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Intuitively, assuming that $F$ is continuous, regardless of how small of a neighborhood $V$ is chosen around the output $F(p)$, a sufficiently small neighborhood $U$ exists around $p$ that is mapped to the inside of
	$V$. 
	
