	If $F:M\to N$ is continuous and bijective, and $F^{-1}$ is continuous, then $F$ is called a homeomorphism. Moreover, if $\forall\,p\in  M,\,\exists\,U\in\mathcal{N}(p)$ such that $F(U)\in\mathcal{T}_N$, then $\left.F\right|_U$ is called a local homeomorphism. It is said that a pair of topological spaces are (locally) homeomorphic if there exists a (local) homeomorphism between them.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Now, provided that $s:\mathbb{N}\to M$ is any sequence, another sequence derived via composition can be built as given by $F(s):n\in\mathbb{N}\mapsto F(s_n)$. If $s\to p\implies F(s)\to F(p)$, then $F$ is said to be sequentially continuous.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Suppose $F$ is continuous and $s\to p$. Upon letting $V\in\mathcal{N}(F(p))$, it directly follows from continuity of $F$ that $p\in F^{-1}(V)\in\mathcal{T}_M$. By supposition, $\exists\,m\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $n\geq m\implies s_n\in F^{-1}(V)$, which further implies $F(s_n)\in V$, and thus $F(s)\to F(p)$. More concisely, continuity implies sequential continuity.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\ 
	Provided that $(M,\mathcal{T}_M)$ is first countable and $F:M\to N$ is sequentially continuous, fix $p\in M$. Let $\{U_k\}_{k\in\mathbb{N}}$ be a countable neighborhood basis at $p$ such that $U_{k+1}\subseteq U_k$ for all $k$, and let $V\in\mathcal{N}(F(p))$. For each $k\in\mathbb{N}$, either $F(U_k)\subseteq V$, in which case $\exists\,U_k\in\mathcal{N}(p)$ satisfying $F(U_k)\subseteq V$, or $\exists\,s_k\in U_k$ such that $F(s_k)\notin V$. In the latter case, for all $k\in\mathbb{N}$, define a sequence $s:n\in\mathbb{N}\mapsto s_n\in U_n$. Given any $U\in\mathcal{N}(p),\,\exists\,m\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $U_m\subseteq U$, hence $s_n\in U$ for all $n\geq m$, implying $s\to p$. By sequential continuity, $F(s)\to F(p)$, and since $V\in\mathcal{N}(F(p)),\,\exists\,m'\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $n\geq m'\implies F(s_n)\in V$. Therefore, for $k=m'$, there does not exist any $q\in U_k$ such that $F(q)\notin V$, and thus $F(U_k)\subseteq V$. Since $V$ was arbitrary, $F$ is continuous at $p$. In other words, sequential continuity of $F$ implies continuity of $F$ in a first-countable space.
	%Provided that $(M,\mathcal{T}_M)$ is first-countable and $F$ is sequentially continuous, assume (apriori) that $F$ is not continuous at $p\in M$. Then $\exists\, V\in\mathcal{N}(F(p))$ such that $U\in\mathcal{N}(p)\implies\exists\,q\in U$, where $F(q)\not\in V$. From first-countability, $\exists\,U_k\subseteq U$, such that $U=\left\{U_k\right\}_{k\in I}$ and $U_{k+1}\subseteq U_k$, where $k\in\mathbb{N}$ and $I\subseteq\mathbb{N}$. Now, construct the sequence $s:\mathbb{N}\to M$ such that for each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, one has $s_n\in U_n$, where $F(s_n)\not\in V$. Because $s_n\in U_n\subseteq U_m\subseteq U$ for $n\geq m$, it follows that $s\to p$. By sequential continuity, $F(s)\to F(p)$, and so for sufficiently large $n$, $F(s_n)\in V$, implying $F$ is continuous, which is a contradiction. Therefore, provided that $(M,\mathcal{T}_M)$ is first-countable, if $F$ is sequentially continuous, it must be continuous. 
	
	\section*{Metric Spaces}
	From scratch, let $M\neq\varnothing$ be a collection of elements. Suppose $M$ admits a distance function $d:M\times M\to\mathbb{R}$. If the function $d$ has the following properties, then it is a metric, and $(M,d)$ is called a metric space:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item $\forall\,x,y\in M,\,d(x,y)\geq0$ -------------------------------------------------- Strict Non-negativity ------------------------
		\item If $x,y\in M$, then $d(x,y)=0$ iff $x=y$ ------------------------------ Indistinguishability 
		---------------------------
		\item $\forall\,x,y\in M,\,d(x,y)=d(y,x)$ ------------------------------------------ Symmetrical ------------------------------------
		\item $\forall\,x,y,z\in M,\,d(x,y)\leq d(x,z)+d(y,z)$ --------------------------- Triangle Inequality -----------------------------
	\end{itemize}
	\vspace{1 mm}
	Intuitively, the metric quantifies how close any two points are with respect to a specific distance convention. Given this structure, one can construct a definition for open balls at points. In particular, provided that $\varepsilon>0$, the open $\varepsilon$-ball at $x\in M$ is the set given by $B_{\varepsilon}(x)=\left\{y\in M\;|\;d(x,y)<\varepsilon\right\}$. %Likewise, the $\varepsilon$-shell at $x\in M$ is given by $S_{\varepsilon}(x)=\partial B_{\varepsilon}(x)=\left\{y\in M\;|\;d(x,y)=\varepsilon\right\}$.
	
	\section*{Properties of Metric Spaces}
	With a metric space at hand, a topology on $M$ can be constructed. Specifically, upon defining the set $\mathcal{T}_d=\left\{U\subseteq M\;|\;\forall\,x\in U,\,\exists\,\epsilon>0:B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U\right\}$, it can be identified as a topology. That is to say, given $U\subseteq M$, if all points in $U$ are individually equipped with at least one ball that resides entirely inside $U$, then the subset $U$ can be considered to be open. The proof is straight forward:
	\begin{itemize}
		\item By inspection, $\varnothing\in\mathcal{T}_d$. Likewise, for any $x\in M$, choose $\varepsilon>0$. It follows that $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subset M$, which implies $M\in\mathcal{T}_d$. Therefore, it consequently holds that $\varnothing,M\in\mathcal{T}_d$.
		\item Contextually, let $\left\{U_i\right\}_{i\in I}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_d$, and define $U=\bigcup_{i\in I}U_i$. Take $x\in U$, and so there necessarily exists some $k\in I$ such that $x\in U_k\in\mathcal{T}_d$. Thus, $\exists\,\varepsilon>0$ with $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U_k\subseteq U$, which implies $U\in\mathcal{T}_d$.
		\item Let $U_1,U_2\in\mathcal{T}_d$, and take $x\in U_1\cap U_2$, which implies $x\in U_1,U_2$. There necessarily exists $\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2>0$ such that $U_1\subseteq B_{\varepsilon_1}(x)$ and $U_2\subseteq B_{\varepsilon_2}(x)$. Take $\varepsilon=\min(\varepsilon_1,\varepsilon_2)$, which implies $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U_1\cap U_2$, further implying $U_1\cap U_2\in\mathcal{T}_d$.
	\end{itemize}
	Consequently, every metric space induces a topological space. Now, consider two points $x\neq y$, and thus $d(x,y)>0$. Let $\varepsilon=\tfrac{1}{3}d(x,y)$, and suppose $z\in B_{\varepsilon}(x)\cap B_{\varepsilon}(y)$. It follows that $d(x,z),d(y,z)<\varepsilon$, and so by the triangle inequality, $d(x,y)\leq d(x,z)+d(y,z)<2\varepsilon=\tfrac{2}{3}d(x,y)$, which is impossible. Therefore, $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\cap B_{\varepsilon}(y)=\varnothing$, which implies $(M,\mathcal{T}_d)$ is Hausdorff. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Next, define the (countable) unit ball system at $x\in M$ to be $\mathcal{N}_B(x)=\left\{B_{1/n}(x)\;|\;n\in\mathbb{N}\right\}$, in which it follows that $\mathcal{N}_B(x)\subseteq\mathcal{T}_d$. Suppose $U\in\mathcal{N}(x)$, and note $\exists\,\varepsilon>0$ such that $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U$. Likewise, $\exists\,n\in\mathbb{N}$ such that $1/n<\varepsilon$, which implies $B_{1/n}(x)\subseteq B_{\varepsilon}(x)$. Therefore, $B_{1/n}(x)\subseteq U$, and thus $\mathcal{N}_B(x)$, which is countable, generates $\mathcal{N}(x)$. It can now be concluded $(M,\mathcal{T}_d)$ is first-countable.   
	
	\section*{Euclidean Space}
	For some $n\in\mathbb{N}$, consider the collection of $n$-tuples $\mathbb{R}^n=\left\{x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)\;|\;x_i\in\mathbb{R},\,i\in\mathbb{N}_n\right\}$. Using a metric, a notion of distance between points can be established. In particular, if $x=(x_1,\dots,x_n)$, by definition, $||x||=\sqrt{\sum_{i=1}^nx_i^2}$, which is the standard (Euclidean) norm on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Upon inspection, the distance function given by $d:(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^n\times\mathbb{R}^n\mapsto||x-y||$ is a valid metric on $\mathbb{R}^n$. Therefore, $(\mathbb{R}^n,d)$ is a metric space which induces the Hausdorff first-countable topological space $(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathcal{T}_d)$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Now, construct the basis of $\mathbb{R}^n$ to be $\mathcal{B}_d=\left\{B_{\varepsilon}(q)\;|\;q\in\mathbb{Q}^n,\,\varepsilon\in\mathbb{Q}^+\right\}$. Suppose $U\subset\mathbb{R}^n$ is open, and let $x\in U$. Then $\exists\,\varepsilon>0$ such that $B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U$. Choose $q\in\mathbb{Q}^n$ such that $d(x,q)<\varepsilon/2$, and take $r\in\mathbb{Q}^+$, where $r<\varepsilon/2$. Then $B_r(q)\subseteq B_{\varepsilon}(x)\subseteq U$. Because $\mathbb{Q}^n$ is countable, $\mathcal{B}_d$ is countable. Therefore, $\mathcal{B}_d$ is a countable basis on $\mathcal{T}_d$, which implies $(\mathbb{R}^n,\mathcal{T}_d)$ is second-countable.
	
