	\section*{Local Coordinate Charts}
	Contextually, if there exists a local homeomorphism $\varphi:U\subseteq M\to\mathbb{R}^n$, which establishes a coordinate system on $M$, then $U$ and $\mathbb{R}^n$ are locally homeomorphic, and $M$ is locally Euclidean on $U$ by definition. To be precise, the pairing $(U,\varphi)$ is referred to as a (local) coordinate chart on $M$. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\\
	Assume (apriori) that $M$ is locally homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\mathbb{R}^m$ ($n,m\in\mathbb{N}$), with respective coordinate charts $\varphi$ and $\psi$. Upon fixing $p\in M,\,\exists\,U,V\in\mathcal{N}(p)$. Provided that $W=U\,\cap\,V$, it follows that $\left.\varphi\right|_W$ and $\left.\psi\right|_W$ are local homeomorphisms onto open sets in $N$. Thus, the map $\psi\circ \varphi^{-1}:\varphi(W)\to\psi(W)$ is a local homeomorphism (bijection). Therefore, $\varphi(W)\cong\psi(W)$, which implies $\mathbb{R}^n\cong\mathbb{R}^m$, further implying $n=m$.    
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	This uniqueness motivates the following definition. If $M$ is locally homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^n$, then it is locally Euclidean of dimension $n$ (i.e. $\mathrm{dim}(M)=n$). Note that in the literature, $\mathrm{dim}(M)$ has a compatible, but more primitive definition in which $\mathrm{dim}(M)=n\rlap{$\quad\not$}\implies M$ is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{R}^n$.
	
