	\section*{Tangent Spaces}
	At every point $p\in M$, by construction, there exists a vector space over $\mathbb{R}$ called the tangent space at $p$ denoted by $T_pM$ with a basis $\left\{\bm{e}_1(p),\cdots,\bm{e}_n(p)\right\}$. The tangent bundle on $M$ is defined as $TM\coloneq\bigcup_{p \in M}\left\{p\right\}\times T_p M$. One natural choice of basis on $T_pM$ is $\bm{e}_i(p)=\left.\partial_i\right|_p$ as will become evident later.  
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	If $\bm{v}_p\in T_pM$, then it is called a tangent vector at $p$. Moreover, provided that $\bm\pi:\bm{v}_p\in T_pM\mapsto p$, the collection of smooth vector fields over $M$ can be constructed as $\Gamma(TM)\coloneq\left\{\bm{X}:M\to TM\,|\,\bm\pi\circ\bm{X}=\mathrm{id}_M, \bm{X}\;\text{smooth}\right\}$.
	
