	\section*{Parallel Transport}
	As each point in $M$ has a distinct tangent space, parallelism between vectors in separate tangent spaces is not well defined. To remedy this, first define (for convenience) $\bm{e}_i^{\gamma}:t\in I\mapsto\bm{e}_i(\gamma(t))$, and consider the tangent vectors $\bm{v}_a=v^i_a\bm{e}_i^{\gamma}(a)\in T_{\gamma(a)}M$ and $\bm{v}_b=v^i_b\bm{e}_i^{\gamma}(b)\in T_{\gamma(b)}M$, where $v^i_a,v^i_b\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $a,b\in I$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Then, define the parallel transport map $\mathcal{P}$ to be a one to one linear map that connects tangent spaces along a curve. In particular, by definition, provided that $\mathcal{P}_{a,b}^{\gamma}:T_{\gamma(a)}M\to T_{\gamma(b)}M$ for any $a,b\in I$, in accordance with $\left\{\bm{e}_i\right\}$, $\exists\,(\mathcal{P}^{\gamma})_i^j:I\times I\to\mathbb{R}$ such that:
	\begin{equation*}
