	\section*{Cotangent Spaces}
	By construction, suppose that at every point $p\in M$ there exists a complimentary vector (cotangent) space at $p$ denoted by $T^*_pM$ with a co-coordinate basis $\left\{\bm{e}^1_p,\cdots,\bm{e}^n_p\right\}$. This is a dual basis of covectors/one-forms/linear functionals with the property that $\bm{e}^j_p(\bm{e}_i(p))=\delta_i^j$. The cotangent bundle on $M$ is analogously defined as $T^*M\coloneq\bigcup_{p \in M}\left\{p\right\}\times T_p^*M$. The tangential bundle structure on $M$ is thus given by $\mathscr{T}(M)\coloneq(TM,T^*M)$.  
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Observe that for any $\bm{v}_p\in T_pM$ and $f\in C^{\infty}(M,\mathbb{R})$, one has $\bm{v}_p=\bm{e}_p^i(\bm{v}_p)\,\bm{e}_i(p)$, and so it follows that $\bm{v}_p[f]=\bm{e}_p^i(\bm{v}_p)\,\bm{e}_i(p)[f]=\partial_if(p)\,\bm{e}_p^i(\bm{v}_p)$, which motivates the differential one-form $df:\bm{v}\in TM\mapsto\bm{v}[f]$, which implies $df=\partial_if\bm{e}^i$. Clearly, $dx^j=\partial_ix^j\bm{e}^i=\delta_i^j\bm{e}^i=\bm{e}^j$, which implies $df=\partial_ifdx^i$ as expected.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Provided that $\bm\pi^*:\omega_p\in T_p^*M\mapsto p$, the collection of smooth covector fields over $M$ can be constructed as $\Gamma(T^*M)\coloneq\left\{\theta:M\to T^*M\,|\,\bm\pi^*\circ\theta=\mathrm{id}_M, \theta\;\text{smooth}\right\}$. So, if $\omega=\omega_k\bm{e}^k\in\Gamma(T^*M)$ and $\bm{v}_p=v^j_p\bm{e}_j(p)$, then $\omega(\bm{v}_p)=\omega_k(p)\,\bm{e}_p^k(v_p^j\bm{e}_j(p))=\omega_k(p)v_p^j\bm{e}_p^k(\bm{e}_j(p))=\omega_k(p)v_p^j\delta_j^k=\omega_k(p)v_p^k$. In this respect, given $\bm{V}\in\Gamma(TM)$, in the proper context, the scalar field $\omega(\bm{V}):p\in M\mapsto\omega_k(p)V^k(p)$ can be naturally constructed.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	At this point, tangent vectors can be concretely assigned coordinates with a chart. Given any tangent vector $\bm{v}\in TM$, define the tangent chart $\varphi_*:TM\to\mathbb{R}^n$ by $\varphi_*(\bm{v})\coloneq\left(v^1,\cdots,v^n\right)$, where $v^i=\bm{e}^i_{\bm\pi\circ\bm{v}}(\bm{v})$.
	
	\section*{Connection 1-Forms}
	Looking back, it may seem as though in order to specify the connection $\nabla$, one would need a function of the form $\tilde{\Gamma}:M\times\mathbb{N}_n^3\to\mathbb{R},(p\,;i,j,k)\mapsto\Gamma_{ij}^k(p)$ to be established as the connection coefficients induce the connection. However, this is not entirely true. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Define the connection one-forms to be $\Gamma_i^k\coloneq\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\bm{e}^j$, implying $\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\bm{e}_i=u^j\,\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\bm{e}_k=\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\bm{e}^j(\bm{u})\,\bm{e}_k=\Gamma_i^k(\bm{u})\,\bm{e}_k$, which further implies, because $\left\{\bm{e}_i\right\}$ is linearly independent, the connection one-forms completely determine the structure of $\nabla$. Thus, one could instead use a function of the form $\tilde\Gamma_*:TM\times\mathbb{N}_n^2\to\mathbb{R},(\bm{u}\,;i,k)\mapsto\Gamma_i^k(\bm{u})$, which packages the information more compactly. To be clear, contextually, one can write:
	\begin{center}
		\large
		$(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\bm{V})^k=\bm{u}\left[V^k\right]+\Gamma_i^k(\bm{u})V^i\quad\text{and}\quad\dot{V}_{\gamma}^k+\Gamma_i^k(\dot\gamma)V_{\gamma}^i=0\quad\text{(for parallel transport)}$ 
	\end{center}
	
	\section*{Connecting the Cotangent Bundle}
	In pursuit of extending the connection $\nabla$ into being applicable to $\Gamma(T^*M)$, it is natural to construct the dual parallel transport map $\mathcal{P}^*$ contextually defined by $(\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{a,b})^*:\omega\in T^*_{\gamma(b)}M\mapsto\omega\circ\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{a,b}\in T^*_{\gamma(a)}M$. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	The map $\mathcal{P}^{\gamma}_{a,b}$ pulls back covectors from $\gamma(a)$ to $\gamma(b)$ with respect to the curve mapping $\gamma$. This establishes a parallelism convention on $T^*M$, enabling covectors to be compared on $M$ analogously to tangent vectors.   
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Suppose that $\omega\in\Gamma(T^*M)$ in the sense that $\omega_p\in T_p^*M$ for each $p\in M$. Now, in the appropriate context, let $\dot\gamma=\bm{u}(\gamma)$ such that $\gamma(0)=p$, and let $\bm{V}_\gamma$ be the curve restriction of $\bm{V}\in\Gamma(TM)$. Then, define the covariant derivative of $\omega$ in the $\bm{u}$ direction at $p$ to be $(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\omega)_p\coloneq\left.\frac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}(\mathcal{P}_{0,t}^\gamma)^*\omega_{\gamma(t)}$. Thus, via the auxiliary field $\bm{V}$:
	\begin{align*}
		\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,&\omega\,(\bm{V}(p))=\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\dfrac{(\mathcal{P}_{0,\delta t}^{\gamma})^*\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(0))-\omega_{\gamma(0)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(0))}{\delta t}=\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\dfrac{\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}\left(\mathcal{P}_{0,\delta t}^{\gamma}(\bm{V}_{\gamma}(0))\right)-\omega_{\gamma(0)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(0))}{\delta t}
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\dfrac{\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}\left(\mathcal{P}_{0,\delta t}^{\gamma}(\bm{V}_{\gamma}(0))\right)-\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(\delta t))}{\delta t}+\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\dfrac{\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(\delta t))-\omega_{\gamma(0)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(0))}{\delta t}
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}\left(\dfrac{\mathcal{P}_{0,\delta t}^{\gamma}(\bm{V}_{\gamma}(0))-\bm{V}_\gamma(\delta t)}{\delta t}\right)+\left.\dfrac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\omega_{\gamma(t)}(\bm{V}_\gamma(t))
		\\[4 mm]
		&=-\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\omega_{\gamma(\delta t)}\left(\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{0,\delta t}\left(\dfrac{\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{\delta t,0}(\bm{V}_\gamma(\delta t))-\bm{V}_\gamma(0)}{\delta t}\right)\right)+\left.\dfrac{d}{dt}\right|_{t=0}\omega(\bm{V})(\gamma(t))
		\\[4 mm]
		&=-\omega_{\gamma(0)}\left(\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{0,0}\left(\lim\limits_{\delta t\to0}\dfrac{\mathcal{P}^\gamma_{\delta t,0}(\bm{V}_\gamma(\delta t))-\bm{V}_\gamma(0)}{\delta t}\right)\right)+\left.D_{\dot\gamma}\right|_p\omega(\bm{V})\quad(\text{due to continuity over}\;\omega\;\text{and}\;\mathcal{P})
		\\[4 mm]
		&=-\omega_p(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\bm{V}(p))+\bm{u}[\omega(\bm{V})](p)\quad\text{(or equivalently)}\quad\nabla_{\bm{u}}(\omega(\bm{V}))(p)-\omega(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\bm{V})(p)
	\end{align*}
	\begin{center}
		\vspace{2 mm}
		\begin{Large}
			$\boxed{\;\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\omega\,(\bm{V})=\nabla_{\bm{u}}(\omega(\bm{V}))-\omega(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\bm{V})\;}$
		\end{Large}
		\vspace{2 mm}\\
		\large
		The Induced Leibniz Rule
	\end{center}
	\vspace{1 mm}
	It will be noted the above formula for $\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\omega$ does not depend on the derivatives of $\bm{V}$ despite appearances. This is especially clear from both the limit construction and the local coordinate expansion given as such:
	\begin{align*}
		\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\omega\,(\bm{V})&=\nabla_{\bm{u}}(\omega(\bm{V}))-\omega(\nabla_{\bm{u}}\bm{V})=u^j\partial_j\left(\omega_kV^k\right)-\omega\left(u^j\left(\partial_jV^i+\Gamma_{ij}^kV^i\right)\bm{e}_k\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		&=u^j\partial_j\,\omega_kV^k+u^j\omega_k\partial_jV^k-\omega_ku^j\left(\partial_jV^i+\Gamma_{ij}^kV^i\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		\text{(index swap)}\qquad&=u^j\partial_j\,\omega_iV^i+u^j\omega_k\partial_jV^k-u^j\omega_k\partial_jV^i-u^j\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\omega_kV^i
		\\[4 mm]
		&=u^j\left(\partial_j\,\omega_i-\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\omega_k\right)V^i\quad\text{where}\quad V^i=\bm{e}^i(\bm{V})\quad\text{with shorthand}\quad(\nabla_j\,\omega)_i=\partial_j\,\omega_i-\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\omega_k
	\end{align*}
	\begin{center}
		\vspace{2 mm}
		\begin{Large}
			$\boxed{\;\nabla_{\bm{u}}\,\omega=u^j\left(\partial_j\,\omega_i-\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\omega_k\right)\bm{e}^i\;}\quad\boxed{\;\nabla_j\,\bm{e}^k=-\Gamma_{ij}^k\,\bm{e}^i\;}$
		\end{Large}
		\begin{large}
			\vspace{2 mm}\\
			The Covariant Derivative on Covector Fields
		\end{large}
	\end{center}
	\vspace{1 mm}
	If not clear, the connection on $T^*M$ denoted by$\left.\nabla\right|_{T^*M}$ has an equivalent algebraic structure to that of the connection on $TM$ denoted by $\left.\nabla\right|_{TM}$. To be specific, the connection $\left.\nabla\right|_{T^*M}$ obeys the Leibniz rule and is $\mathbb{R}$-linear in the second (right) slot, and is $C^{\infty}(M,\mathbb{R})$-linear in the first (lower) slot analogously to $\left.\nabla\right|_{TM}$. 
	
	
	\section*{Integral (Flow) Curves}
	In light of the fact that every curve mapping $\gamma$ can be assigned a vector field via its tangents $\dot\gamma$, it ought to be that every vector field can be assigned (a congruence of) curve mappings. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	The flow of a vector field is said to be a curve whose tangents are the vector field itself. In this respect, by definition, the integral curve (flow) mapping of $\bm{u}\in\Gamma(TM)$ initially passing through $p\in M$ is denoted by $\phi_{(\cdot)}^{\bm{u}}(p):I_p\subseteq\mathbb{R}\to M$, where $0\in I_p$, and is uniquely determined by the IVP over $I_p$ as given below:
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{large}
		\begin{center}
			$\boxed{\;\dfrac{d}{dt}\left(\varphi\circ\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p)\right)=\varphi_*(\bm{u})\circ\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\phi_0^{\bm{u}}(p)=p\;}$
			\vspace{2 mm}\\
			Integral Curve Equation
		\end{center}
	\end{large}
	\vspace{2 mm}
	Due to the uniqueness of the solution to the above IVP, the flow (map) of $\bm{u}$ given by $\phi_t^{\bm{u}}:p\in M\mapsto\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p)\in M$ is a diffeomorphism with an inverse $\phi_{-t}^{\bm{u}}$. Importantly, because $\varphi$ and $\varphi_*$ are local isomorphisms, the integral curve solution to the above IVP remains invariant under the (symbolic) coordinate swap $(\varphi,\varphi_*)\to(\bbar\varphi,\bbar\varphi_*)$. 
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	It is also noteworthy to mention that the flow curves generated by the basis vectors pertaining to a particular coordinate system are the coordinate grid lines mapped (via $\varphi^{-1}$) from the chart onto the manifold.
	
