	Upon repeatedly (directionally) differentiating the integral curve equation, via induction, it follows that  for each $k\in\mathbb{N}_0$, $\frac{d^k}{dt^k}\left(\varphi\circ\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p)\right)\left.\right|_{t=0}=D_{\bm{u}}^k\,\varphi\left(p\right)$, which implies by Taylor's Theorem:
	\begin{equation*}
		\varphi\left(\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p)\right)=\sum\limits_{k=0}^N\dfrac{t^k}{k!}D_{\bm{u}}^k\,\varphi\left(p\right)+\dfrac{1}{N!}\int_0^t(t-\tau)^ND_{\bm{u}}^{N+1}\,\varphi\left(\phi_{\tau}^{\bm{u}}(p)\right)d\tau=\varphi(p)+t\varphi_*(\bm{u}(p))+\dots
	\end{equation*}
	In this respect, if $\bm{u}$ is analytic on $M$, or equivalently, $\phi^{\bm{u}}(p)$ is analytic on $I_p$ for all $p\in M$, then for each $p\in M$, $\exists\,\varepsilon_p>0$ such that $\forall\,t\in(-\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_p)$, upon taking the limit as $N\to\infty$ (above), one could write the flow (map) of $\bm{u}$ more compactly via the exponential as follows:
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{center}
		$\boxed{\;\phi_t^{\bm{u}}=\varphi^{-1}\, e^{t\bm{u}}\,\varphi\quad\text{s.t.}\quad e^{t\bm{u}}\coloneq\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\infty}\dfrac{t^k}{k!}D^k_{\bm{u}}\;}$
		\vspace{1.5 mm}\\
		The Analytic Flow Mapping
	\end{center}
	\vspace{2 mm}
	In this light, given $f\in C^{\infty}(M,\mathbb{R})$, via Taylor's Theorem, $f(\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p))=\sum_{k=0}^N\frac{t^k}{k!}D^k_{\bm{u}}f(p)+\mathcal{O}(t^{N+1})$, and so if $f$ is analytic, then for each $p\in M$, $\exists\,\varepsilon_p>0$ such that $\forall\,t\in(-\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_p)$, upon taking the limit as $N\to\infty$, one has $f(\phi_t^{\bm{u}}(p))=e^{t\bm{u}}f(p)$. The flow's pullback will be consistently (futuristically) given by $(\phi_t^{\bm{u}})^*=e^{t\bm{u}}$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	In any case, observe that if $\bm{u}[f]=0$ on $M$, then $\frac{d}{dt}f(\phi_t^{u}(p))=0$ for all respective $p\in M$ and $t\in I_p$. In other words, $f$ is conserved on each integral curve of $\bm{u}$. That is, $f(\phi_{I_p}^{\bm{u}}(p))$ is constant for all respective $p\in M$.
	
	\section*{The Commutator}
	By inspection the flows of vector fields do not commute. That is, given $\bm{X},\bm{Y}\in\Gamma(TM)$ and $\varepsilon_x,\varepsilon_y\in\mathbb{R}$, $\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}\neq\phi_{\varepsilon_
		y}^{\bm{Y}}\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}$ in general. In order to quantify this anti-commutativity, without loss of generality, assume $\bm{X}$ and $\bm{Y}$ are analytic, which implies $\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}=(\varphi^{-1}\, e^{\varepsilon_x\bm{X}}\,\varphi)(\varphi^{-1}\,e^{\varepsilon_y\bm{Y}}\,\varphi)=\varphi^{-1}\,e^{\varepsilon_x\bm{X}}\,e^{\varepsilon_y\bm{Y}}\,\varphi$. Therefore:
	\begin{equation*}
		\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}=\varphi^{-1}\, e^{\varepsilon_x\bm{X}+\varepsilon_y\bm{Y}}\,\varphi\quad\text{s.t.}\quad e^{\varepsilon_x\bm{X}+\varepsilon_y\bm{Y}}\coloneq\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\infty}\dfrac{\left(\varepsilon_xD_{\bm{X}}+\varepsilon_yD_{\bm{Y}}\right)^k}{k!}=\sum\limits_{k=0}^{\infty}\dfrac{1}{k!}\sum\limits_{j=0}^k\binom{k}{j}\varepsilon_x^j\varepsilon_y^{k-j}D_{\bm{X}}^jD_{\bm{Y}}^{k-j}
	\end{equation*}
	\\
	Thus, regardless of analyticity, the commuted flow of $\bm{X}$ and $\bm{Y}$ can be truncated to second order on the chart given as $\varphi\circ\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}=\left(1+(\varepsilon_xD_{\bm{X}}+\varepsilon_yD_{\bm{Y}})+\frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_x^2D_{\bm{X}}^2+2\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_yD_{\bm{X}}D_{\bm{Y}}+\varepsilon_y^2D_{\bm{Y}}^2)\right)\varphi+\mathcal{O}\left((\varepsilon_x,\varepsilon_y)^3\right)$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	With the understanding that $D_{\bm{X}}$ and $D_{\bm{Y}}$ generally do not commute, take particular note of the symmetry in the first order term versus the anti-symmetry in the second order term between their ordering. In this regard, the coordinate difference between the anti-commuted flows of $\bm{X}$ and $\bm{Y}$ is given as such: 
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{center}
		$\varphi\circ\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}-\varphi\circ\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}=\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_y\left(D_{\bm{X}}D_{\bm{Y}}-D_{\bm{Y}}D_{\bm{X}}\right)\varphi+\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_y\mathcal{O}(\varepsilon_x,\varepsilon_y)$
		\vspace{4 mm}\\
		----- which motivates the definition -----
		\vspace{4 mm}\\
		\large
		$\boxed{\;[\bm{X,\bm{Y}}][\cdot]\coloneq(D_{\bm{X}}D_{\bm{Y}}-D_{\bm{Y}}D_{\bm{X}})(\cdot)=\bm{X}[\bm{Y}[\cdot]]-\bm{Y}[\bm{X}[\cdot]]\quad\text{on}\quad C^2(M,\mathbb{R})\;}$
		\vspace{1.5 mm}\\
		The Commutator (Lie Bracket)
	\end{center}
	\vspace{2 mm}
	The commutator is a (second order) local measure of the failure for anti-commuted vector field flows to close. Also, observe $[\bm{X},\bm{Y}][\cdot]$ is a linear differential operator, implying $[\bm{X},\bm{Y}]$ corresponds to a unique vector field.
	
