	Using local coordinates, the commutator can be explicitly expanded with the (coordinate frame) convention that $\bm{e}_i[\cdot]=\partial_i(\cdot)$, which intuitively implies $[\bm{e}_i,\bm{e}_j]=0$ (see Appendix A). Upon coordinate expanding:
	\vspace{1 mm}
	\begin{align*}
		[\bm{X},\bm{Y}][\cdot]&=X^i\partial_i\left(Y^j\partial_j(\cdot)\right)-Y^j\partial_j\left(X^i\partial_i(\cdot)\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\left(X^i\partial_iY^j\partial_j(\cdot)+X^iY^j\partial_i\partial_j(\cdot)\right)-\left(Y^j\partial_jX^i\partial_i(\cdot)+Y^jX^i\partial_j\partial_i(\cdot)\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\left(X^i\partial_iY^j\partial_j(\cdot)-Y^j\partial_jX^i\partial_i(\cdot)\right)+X^iY^j\left(\partial_i\partial_j(\cdot)-\partial_j\partial_i(\cdot)\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\left(X^i\partial_iY^j\partial_j(\cdot)-Y^i\partial_iX^j\partial_j(\cdot)\right)+X^iY^j\left(\partial_i\partial_j(\cdot)-\partial_i\partial_j(\cdot)\right)
		\\[4 mm]
		&=\left(X^i\partial_iY^j-Y^i\partial_iX^j\right)\partial_j(\cdot)\quad\text{(or equivalently)}\quad\left(X^i\partial_iY^j-Y^i\partial_iX^j\right)\bm{e}_j[\cdot]
	\end{align*}
	\begin{center}
		\vspace{1.25 mm}
		\begin{large}
			$\boxed{\;[\bm{X},\bm{Y}]=\left(X^i\partial_iY^j-Y^i\partial_iX^j\right)\bm{e}_j\;}$
		\end{large}
		\vspace{2 mm}\\
		Local Coordinate Expansion of the Commutator\phantom{00} 
	\end{center}
	
	\section*{Closing the Gap}
	In general, for any $\bm{X},\bm{Y}\in\Gamma(TM)$, in light of the fact that $ \phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}\neq\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}$ with a (second order) closure discrepancy proportional to $\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_y$, there exists a closure field $\bm{C}\in\Gamma(TM)$ such that $\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}=\phi_{\varepsilon_x\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{C}}\phi_{\varepsilon_y}^{\bm{Y}}\phi_{\varepsilon_x}^{\bm{X}}$. Although $\bm{C}$ is not uniquely determined, it is constrained to first order in $\varepsilon_x$ and $\varepsilon_y$.
	
