	Before directly addressing the pushforward, a means of transforming functions on $N$ to functions on $M$ with respect to $F$ ought to be developed first. Define the pullback of $F$ denoted by $F^*:C^{\infty}(N,\mathbb{R})\to C^{\infty}(M,\mathbb{R})$ to be in accordance with $F^*f=f\circ F$. Observe the linear structure here and in the successive operations.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	As there is a unique correspondence between directional differential operators and tangent vectors, the pushforward can be defined implicitly as follows. On a point-wise basis, given any tangent vector $\bm{v}\in TM$, let $F_*(\bm{v}):f\in C^{\infty}(N,\mathbb{R})\mapsto\bm{v}[F^*f]\in\mathbb{R}$.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	Given local coordinate functions $y^i$ on $N$ and a coordinate frame $\left\{\bm{\varepsilon}_i\right\}$ on $TN$, the pushforward can be explicitly computed. In particular, provided that $F^i=y^i\circ F$ and $\bm{v}_p=v^i_p\bm{e}_i(p)$, it thus follows:
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{center}
		$F_*(\bm{v}_p)[f]=\bm{v}_p[F^*f]=v^j_p\partial_j(f\circ F)=v^j_p\left.\dfrac{\partial F^i}{\partial x^j}\right|_p\left.\dfrac{\partial f}{\partial y^i}\right|_{F(p)}=v^j_p\left.\dfrac{\partial F^i}{\partial x^j}\right|_p\bm{\varepsilon}_i(F(p))[f]=\bm{v}_p\left[F^i\right]\bm{\varepsilon}_i(F(p))[f]$
		\vspace{5 mm}\\
		$\implies\boxed{\;F_*(\bm{v}_p)=\bm{v}_p\left[F^i\right]\bm{\varepsilon}_i(F(p))\;}\quad(\text{due to the unique pairing}\;(\bm{z}[\cdot],\bm{z})\;\forall\,\bm{z}\in TN)$
	\end{center}
	\vspace{2 mm}
	Given that $F$ is a diffeomorphism, for any $\bm{X}\in\Gamma(TM)$, let $F_*(\bm{X}):q\in N\mapsto F_*\left(\bm{X}\left(F^{-1}(q)\right)\right)$. In this regard, $[\cdot,\cdot]$ is preserved with respect to $F_*$. That is to say, if $\bm{Y}\in\Gamma(TM)$ as well, then on the one hand:
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{center}
		$F_*([\bm{X},\bm{Y}])[f]=[\bm{X},\bm{Y}][F^*f]=\bm{X}[\bm{Y}[F^*f]]-\bm{Y}[\bm{X}[F^*f]]=\bm{X}[F_*(\bm{Y})[f]\circ F]-\bm{Y}[F_*(\bm{X})[f]\circ F]$
		\vspace{4 mm}\\
		----- whilst on the other hand -----
		\vspace{4 mm}\\
		$[F_*(\bm{X}),F_*(\bm{Y})][f]=F_*(\bm{X})[F_*(\bm{Y)}[f]]-F_*(\bm{Y})[F_*(\bm{X)}[f]]=\bm{X}[F^*(F_*(\bm{Y})[f])]-\bm{Y}[F^*(F_*(\bm{X})[f])]$
		\vspace{5 mm}\\
		$\implies\boxed{\;F_*([\bm{X},\bm{Y}])=[F_*(\bm{X}),F_*(\bm{Y})]\;}\quad(\text{via transitivity as WLOG,}\;\,F^*(F_*(\bm{Y})[f])=F_*(\bm{Y})[f]\circ F)$
	\end{center}
	\vspace{3 mm}
	In order for one-forms in $T^*N$ to act upon tangent vectors in $TM$ with respect to $F$, they can be pulled back similarly to scalar fields on $N$. Maintaining the assumption that $F$ is a diffeomorphism, the pullback on $\omega\in T^*N$ by $F$ is implicitly defined by $F^*\omega:\bm{v}\in T_{F^{-1}(\bm\pi^*\circ\,\omega)}M\mapsto\omega(F_*(\bm{v}))\in\mathbb{R}$. Using coordinates, provided that $\omega_{F(p)}=\omega_i\bm{\varepsilon}^i(F(p))$, it follows:
	\vspace{2 mm}
	\begin{center}
		$F^*\omega_{F(p)}(\bm{e}_j(p))=\omega_{F(p)}\left(\bm{e}_j(p)[F^i]\bm{\varepsilon}_i(F(p))\right)=\omega_{F(p)}\left(\partial_jF^i(p)\bm{\varepsilon}_i(F(p)\right)=\omega_i\partial_jF^i(p)$ 
		\vspace{5 mm}\\
		$\implies\boxed{\;F^*\omega_{F(p)}=\omega_i\partial_jF^i(p)\,\bm{e}^j_p\;}\quad(\text{due to the linearity of}\;\,F^*\omega\;\,\text{on}\;\,TM\;\,\text{and the uniqueness of 1-forms})$
	\end{center}
	
	\section*{Lie Drag}
	At this point, one may think that the only way to compare tangent vectors on $TM$ is via a connection $\nabla$. However, there exists another way using flows of vector fields in conjunction with their pushforward maps.
	\vspace{3 mm}\\
	To be specific, provided that $\bm{X},\bm{Y}\in\Gamma(TM)$, consider how $\bm{Y}$ changes along the flow of $\bm{X}$. That is, in order to compare $\bm{Y}\left(\phi_t^{\bm{X}}(p)\right)$ relative to $\bm{Y}(p)$, push the tangent vector back to $T_pM$ along the flow of $\bm{X}$ using its reversed pushforward $\left(\phi_{-t}^{\bm{X}}\right)_*:\bm{v}\in TM\to T_{\phi_{-t}^{\bm{X}}(\bm\pi\circ\bm{v})}M$. This process is referred to as Lie drag. 
	
